{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>An ox or a sheep, it and its offspring.</b> This law applies to females and not to males, for it is permissible to slaughter the father and the son on one day — since the halakhah follows the one who says we do not concern ourselves with the seed of the father. And this is what Onkelos translated: ותורתא או שיתא — to say that it applies to females and not to males. And according to this law, Scripture should have said ופרה או כשבה אותה ואת בנה (a cow or a ewe, it and its offspring), but because it mentioned שור או כשב או עז (an ox or a sheep or a goat), it mentioned the father and the son. <b>And it shall be seven days under its mother.</b> He says that among the species mentioned here, which are ox and sheep, there is yet another commandment: for 'it and its offspring' mentioned above — you shall not slaughter them on one day. Thus did the Ramban, of blessed memory, explain it." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״ושור או שה אותו ואת בנו לא תשחטו ביום אחד״ — this is what Scripture says (Proverbs 12): ״יודע צדיק נפש בהמתו ורחמי רשעים אכזרי״. ״יודע צדיק נפש בהמתו״ — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who said ״ושור או שה אותו ואת בנו לא תשחטו ביום אחד״; ״ורחמי רשעים אכזרי״ — this is the wicked Haman, who wrote להשמיד להרוג ולאבד. ״יודע צדיק נפש בהמתו״ — this is what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 22): ״לא תקח האם על הבנים״; ״ורחמי רשעים אכזרי״ — this is the wicked Sennacherib, about whom it is written (Hosea 10): ״אם על בנים רטשה״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:40.745552004Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}